import OrlikSolomonBook.Node
import OrlikSolomon

open Verso.Genre Manual
open OrlikSolomonBook

#doc (Manual) "Projective complements" =>
%%%
tag := "projective"
file := "projective"
%%%

The projective theorem is deduced from the affine theorem, applied to the central cone $`c\mathcal{P}`.
Choosing $`H_0\in\mathcal{P}` with equation $`\ell_0`, the complement $`V_{\mathcal{P}}` of the cone splits as $`U_{\mathcal{P}}\times\mathbb{G}_m`, with $`\ell_i=t(\ell_i/\ell_0)`.
So $`[\ell_i]=[t]+[\ell_i/\ell_0]`, and the differences $`[\ell_i]-[\ell_j]` come from $`U_{\mathcal{P}}`.

On the algebra side, a central arrangement has only nonempty intersections, so all its relators are boundaries, and the boundary $`\partial` descends to the quotient.
The projective algebra is its kernel.
The argument is specific to central arrangements: in general, the boundary does not descend through the empty-intersection relators.

The decomposition $`b+e_0c` of the central algebra is a statement about modules.
Multiplicatively it is a _graded_ tensor product with an exterior generator, and an ordinary tensor product of algebras would impose the wrong multiplication, without the sign.

The empty projective arrangement is not covered.
There is no hyperplane at infinity to choose, the space is $`\mathbb{P}^n` itself, and its calculation needs a projective bundle formula that is not among the axioms.

:::node "def:projective"
A split projective arrangement is a finite set of codimension-one linear subspaces of a vector space of dimension $`n+1`, regarded as hyperplanes in its projectivization.
For $`H_0\in\mathcal{P}` with equation $`\ell_0`, the slice $`\ell_0=1` is an affine $`n`-space, and intersecting the other hyperplanes with it gives the decone arrangement.
:::

:::node "lem:decone"
The projective complement is the decone complement, and there is an isomorphism $`V_{\mathcal{P}}\simeq U_{\mathcal{P}}\times\mathbb{G}_m` given by $`v\mapsto(v/\ell_0(v),\ell_0(v))` with inverse $`(u,t)\mapsto tu`.
These are identities of regular maps, not merely of rational points.
:::

:::node "lem:central-boundary"
For a central arrangement, the word boundary descends to a $`B`-linear map $`\partial` on the Orlik–Solomon algebra of bidegree $`(-1,-1)`, with $`\partial e_i=1`, $`\partial^2=0` and $`\partial(xy)=\partial(x)y+(-1)^px\partial(y)` for $`x` of cohomological degree $`p`.
:::

:::node "lem:projective-algebra"
For $`A=\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})`, define $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})=\ker\partial`.
It is a graded $`B`-subalgebra, generated by the differences $`e_i-e_0` for any chosen label $`0`, and $`(b,c)\mapsto b+e_0c` is a module equivalence from two copies of it to $`A`, with the second summand shifted by $`(1,1)`.
:::

:::node "lem:gm-product"
For suitable $`X`, with projection $`p:X\times\mathbb{G}_m\to X` and second coordinate $`t`, the map $`(b,c)\mapsto p^*b+[t]\,p^*c` is a $`B`-linear equivalence from $`C(X)\oplus C(X)` to $`C(X\times\mathbb{G}_m)`, with the second summand shifted by $`(1,1)`.
Pullback along the section $`t=1` recovers $`b`, and the residue recovers $`c`.
:::

:::node "thm:projective"
For a nonempty split projective arrangement there is a canonical bigrading-preserving equivalence from $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})` to $`C(U_{\mathcal{P}})`, sending $`e_i-e_j` to $`[\ell_i/\ell_j]`.
It is independent of the equations and of the hyperplane chosen at infinity.
:::

:::node "cor:projective-betti"
The projective Betti polynomial is the decone polynomial and satisfies $`P_{c\mathcal{P}}(t)=(1+t)P_{\mathcal{P}}(t)`.
In particular $`b_1(U_{\mathcal{P}})=|\mathcal{P}|-1`.
:::
